Power simulation (power.R, mcpower.R, psockpower.R, mpipower.R)

The power of a statistical test is the chance that it detects a real effect.
It depends on the sample size and on how big the effect is, and it is often
easiest to estimate by simulation: draw many fake datasets with a known
effect, run the test on each, and count how often it gives p < 0.05.

These examples estimate the power of a two-sample t-test for sample sizes
20, 50, 100 and 200 and effect sizes 0, 0.2, 0.5 and 0.8. Each test draws
both samples from one large shared population and adds the effect to the
second sample. With an effect of 0 there is nothing to detect, so that
column shows the false positive rate, which should be close to 0.05.

Why it parallelizes well: every simulated test is independent, so the tests
are split into tasks that different workers run, and the counts are summed.
The large shared population also shows the cost of copying data to workers
that do not share memory (PSOCK and MPI) compared to forked ones (multicore).
